# Book 4 warm-up content, Chapters 1-20. Every opener in this book is expository, so each new hook goes in
# front of it (no replace). Hook types rotate: scene, puzzle, number, history, question, myth, failure.
C = {
1: dict(hook="Mount a 10 pF capacitor on its pads, feed it 2.45 GHz, and it is no longer a capacitor. Its 0.8 nH of mounted inductance resonates with it at 1.78 GHz, and above that frequency the part behaves as a small inductor. Nothing about the part changed. The frequency did, and that is the whole subject of this book. The capacitor keeps its name on the packet; the circuit has other opinions.",
 meet="Every Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and cellular board, every SMA connector and coax jumper, and every data sheet that quotes power in dBm.",
 bench="Above a few hundred megahertz, check every passive part's self-resonant frequency before you trust its value. A DC-blocking capacitor works best near its series resonance, and a bypass capacitor above it is an inductor, so the 100 nF part from the digital schematic may be the wrong choice on an RF line. Think in dBm from the first day: gains and losses add, and mistakes in milliwatts get caught faster in decibels.",
 use=(["Wavelength λ = c/f, and λ/√εr on a board.", "dBm, dB, and the habit of adding gains and losses.", "Self-resonance of real capacitors and inductors."], ["The full field theory behind why lumped elements fail."])),
2: dict(hook="Clip 2 m of ordinary 50 Ω coax to an oscilloscope and at DC it looks like a 202 pF capacitor. Send a fast edge into it and for the first 20 ns, the time the edge takes to reach the far end and come back, the scope sees a plain 50 Ω resistor. Same cable, two answers. Which one is right depends on whether the edge is shorter than the trip.",
 meet="Every coax cable, PCB trace above a few hundred megahertz, scope probe, and antenna feed, and every VSWR or return-loss number on a data sheet.",
 bench="Learn to convert in your head: VSWR 2 is |Γ| = 1/3, about 9.5 dB return loss and half a decibel of mismatch loss. Most of the time that half decibel isn't the problem. The problem is what the reflection does to the amplifier driving the line. When a reading looks too good, remember that cable loss flatters return loss twice: 3 dB of cable makes a poor antenna look 6 dB better than it is.",
 use=(["Γ = (ZL − Z0)/(ZL + Z0), VSWR, and return loss, and quick conversion between them.", "Delay per meter from the velocity factor.", "Cable loss appears twice in a return-loss measurement."], ["The telegrapher's equations in full.", "Closed-form input impedance of lossy lines."])),
3: dict(hook="A 100 W GaN transistor on a 50 V rail wants to see about 10 Ω. A 28 V transistor of the same power wants about 3. Neither one wants 50, which is what every cable, connector, and antenna offers. A matching network bridges that gap, and it comes with a price tag: the bigger the ratio, the narrower the band.",
 meet="The output of every power amplifier, the input of every LNA, and the two or three small parts between a radio chip and its antenna.",
 bench="Before you design a match, compute its Q. Q = √(R_high/R_low − 1) tells you the bandwidth you can expect and how much the parts' own losses will cost. A 5:1 ratio is easy, while 25:1 in one step is narrow and lossy, so split it into two L-sections. Leave footprints for a pi network even when the design says L: the third pad costs nothing and saves a board spin when the antenna turns out different from its data sheet.",
 use=(["Q = √(R_high/R_low − 1) and the series and shunt reactances from it.", "Two cascaded L-sections for large ratios.", "Inductor Q as the main source of matching loss."], ["Exact bandwidth formulas for multi-element networks."])),
4: dict(hook="In 1939 Phillip Smith, an engineer at Bell Telephone Laboratories, published a chart in Electronics magazine that turned transmission-line arithmetic into geometry. Every network analyzer still draws it. Moving along a line becomes a rotation, adding a shunt part becomes a slide along a circle, and the center of the chart is the match you are steering toward.",
 meet="The network analyzer screen, matching above a few gigahertz, bias tees and quarter-wave chokes, and the stubs and radial pads visible on microwave boards.",
 bench="Use the Smith chart to see a design, not to calculate it; let the software do the arithmetic. What the chart shows at a glance is direction. Clockwise is toward the generator. Series parts move along constant-resistance circles, and shunt parts move along constant-conductance circles. A good habit is to sweep a match and look at the shape it traces: a tight knot around the center means wide bandwidth, a long arc means a narrow one.",
 use=(["Quarter-wave transformer Zq = √(Z0 RL).", "Reading impedance, VSWR, and the direction 'toward generator' on the chart.", "A quarter-wave line that turns a short into an open, for bias feeds."], ["Graphical stub design done fully by hand."])),
5: dict(hook="A VSWR of 1.5 sounds harmless. Put a generator with that VSWR into a power sensor with the same one, and the reading can be off by a third of a decibel either way, with no instrument fault anywhere. S-parameters are the language that makes such numbers visible, and the reason RF engineers never quote a power level without its uncertainty.",
 meet="Every network analyzer measurement, every component data sheet above 100 MHz, every amplifier stability check, and every S2P file you import into a simulator.",
 bench="Calibrate at the plane you care about, not at the instrument's port; the cable and adapter between them are part of the measurement. Check a new transistor's S-parameters for stability over the whole band where it has gain, including far below and above your operating band. Amplifiers usually oscillate somewhere you weren't looking. A quick K and |Δ| check from the S2P file takes a minute and saves a board turn.",
 use=(["|S11| as return loss and |S21| as gain or insertion loss.", "Mismatch uncertainty 20 log(1 ± |Γs||Γl|).", "Rollett's K and |Δ| as the quick stability check."], ["Conversions between S, Z, Y, and ABCD parameters by hand.", "Signal-flow-graph derivations."])),
6: dict(hook="A third-order low-pass filter with a 1 GHz cutoff takes only 18 dB off a 2 GHz harmonic. A transmitter's harmonics usually need to be several tens of decibels down, so that filter alone is not nearly enough. How steep a filter must be, and what its steepness costs in loss and size, is the question every filter design starts with.",
 meet="Harmonic filters after power amplifiers, band filters in front of receivers, the duplexer in every phone, and the waveguide on radar and satellite hardware.",
 bench="Pick the technology from Q before you pick the topology. A microstrip resonator with a Q of 200 costs nearly 2 dB in a narrow filter, a cavity with a Q of 2000 costs a fifth of a decibel, and no amount of optimization makes the microstrip version match the cavity. On a real board, the parasitic inductance of each shunt capacitor's via limits the rejection you can reach, so place a ground via right at each capacitor.",
 use=(["Butterworth vs Chebyshev: flat passband vs steeper skirt.", "Midband loss ≈ 4.343 Σg/(FBW·Qu).", "Waveguide cutoff fc = c/2a."], ["Exact synthesis of the prototype element values.", "Higher-order waveguide mode theory."])),
7: dict(hook="Cut a dipole to a twentieth of a wavelength, give it 2 Ω of wire loss, and half the power you feed it simply heats the wire. Double its length and the efficiency jumps to more than 80 %. Small antennas aren't just weaker; they are inefficient, and that one fact shapes every gadget that radiates.",
 meet="Every phone, router, key fob, satellite dish, and car radar, and every link budget that uses dBi.",
 bench="An antenna data sheet's gain was measured on a reference ground plane, usually larger than yours, with nothing nearby. Your plastic case, battery, and the user's hand all detune it. Budget several decibels for that and tune the match in the final enclosure, not on a bare board. Keep the antenna's keep-out area free of copper on every layer; a ground pour under a chip antenna is one of the most common ways to kill its efficiency.",
 use=(["Gain = efficiency × directivity, in dBi.", "Friis link equation and the 20 log R path loss.", "Dish gain from aperture, and beamwidth ≈ 70λ/D degrees."], ["Radiation integrals and the vector potential."])),
8: dict(hook="How does a flat panel on a 5G mast point its beam at a phone a block away without moving a single part? It delays each element's signal a little more than its neighbor's. With half-wavelength spacing, a phase step of −90° from one element to the next swings the beam 30° off broadside, and the electronics can do that in microseconds.",
 meet="5G and satellite-internet terminals, automotive radar, Wi-Fi beamforming, and every phased-array radar.",
 bench="Grating lobes are the array's version of aliasing. Keep element spacing below about 0.6 λ at the highest frequency and widest scan angle you need, or a second full-strength beam appears where nobody wanted it. Calibration usually limits an array more than the phase-shifter bit count does. Element-to-element gain and phase errors from manufacturing, temperature, and aging raise sidelobes long before 3-bit quantization matters.",
 use=(["Phase step β = −kd sin θ0 for steering.", "Array gain ≈ element gain + 10 log N.", "Grating-lobe limit d/λ < 1/(1 + sin θmax)."], ["Array synthesis by Chebyshev and Taylor weighting."])),
9: dict(hook="The splitter that feeds two televisions from one antenna lead looks like the simplest part in the house. It isn't. Splitting a signal in two without reflecting any back, and without letting one output hear the other, takes either a transformer or, at microwave frequencies, a pair of quarter-wave lines and one well-placed resistor.",
 meet="Antenna splitters and combiners, the power detector's coupler in every transmitter, balanced amplifiers, IQ modulators, and every network analyzer's reflectometer.",
 bench="A coupler's directivity, not its coupling value, decides how well it measures a good match. A coupler with 30 dB directivity can't tell a 20 dB return loss from 17.6 or 23.3 dB. For Wilkinson dividers, choose a small package for the isolation resistor (0402 or smaller) and keep it right at the line ends; its parasitic length is what limits isolation at the top of the band.",
 use=(["Wilkinson: λ/4 lines of √2·Z0 and a 2Z0 resistor.", "Directivity sets the error of a reflected-power reading.", "Quadrature hybrids for balanced amplifiers."], ["Even- and odd-mode analysis in full.", "Proofs of the three-port impossibility theorem."])),
10: dict(hook="On a 0.508 mm laminate with a permittivity of 3.55, a 50 Ω trace is 1.14 mm wide, and the 70.7 Ω line beside it is 0.62 mm. The board house's etching tolerance is a few hundredths of a millimeter, which makes it part of your impedance specification whether you write it down or not.",
 meet="Every RF and high-speed PCB stack-up, controlled-impedance fabrication notes, and the trace widths in any reference layout.",
 bench="Get the stack-up from your fabricator before you draw a single RF trace, and use their dielectric constant at your frequency, not the 1 MHz value in a generic table. FR-4's permittivity and loss both vary between suppliers and with glass weave. Put the impedance requirement and the reference layer on the fabrication drawing and ask for a test coupon; an impedance-controlled order costs little more and removes a whole class of surprises.",
 use=(["Microstrip width from impedance tables or a calculator.", "εeff and guided wavelength on the board.", "Dielectric loss rising with frequency and tan δ."], ["Hammerstad's closed-form expressions in detail.", "Dispersion and higher-order mode formulas."])),
11: dict(hook="In 1950 Robert Fano proved that no lossless network, however clever, can match a reactive load perfectly over a band. A transistor input of 50 Ω in parallel with 5 pF can be matched to 17 dB return loss across 1 GHz, but only to under 9 dB across 2 GHz. That limit isn't a weakness of your design. It is a law.",
 meet="Wideband test equipment, octave-band amplifiers, antenna matching across several cellular bands, and every broadband transformer.",
 bench="Run the Bode-Fano number before promising a bandwidth; it takes a minute and settles arguments. When the limit bites, the honest choices are to accept a little loss (a resistor or a lossy match flattens the band), to use a balanced or feedback amplifier that hides the mismatch, or to switch between narrower matches. Multisection transformers trade length for bandwidth, so check that the extra length fits before you add sections.",
 use=(["Single-section bandwidth for a given maximum reflection.", "The Bode-Fano limit for an RC load.", "Resistive and feedback broadbanding."], ["Chebyshev multisection synthesis by hand."])),
12: dict(hook="A copper cavity filter that's perfect at room temperature can be useless after warming 50 K. Its 1.28 MHz passband drifts 8.5 MHz, more than six times its own width. The same cavity in Invar moves only 0.6 MHz. Whether the filter still works in the sun is decided by the material, not the circuit.",
 meet="Base-station and satellite multiplexers, radar front ends, dielectric-resonator oscillators, and the high-Q filters between a power amplifier and its antenna.",
 bench="Every high-Q resonator is also a thermometer. Ask for the drift in ppm/K and multiply by your temperature range before you look at anything else. Tuning screws raise loss and become a long-term stability risk, so lock them and plan for vibration. When unloaded Q is the specification, surface finish and plating matter more than the drawing suggests; specify the plating and its thickness.",
 use=(["Qu, QL, and external Q, and how coupling sets QL.", "Drift = (ppm/K) × f × ΔT.", "Q scaling with √σ."], ["Field solutions for TE and TM cavity modes."])),
13: dict(hook="Why does every small gadget fight its antenna? Because in 1948 Lan Jen Chu put a ceiling on it. An antenna that fits in a 20 mm sphere at 915 MHz can never cover more than about 3.5 % bandwidth at a 2:1 VSWR, whatever its shape, and real antennas fall short of even that.",
 meet="Chip antennas on IoT boards, key-fob loops, quarter-wave whips over ground planes, and the horns that feed dishes and test ranges.",
 bench="For a small antenna, the ground plane matters as much as the antenna itself. A monopole on a 30 mm board isn't the monopole in the data sheet, because the board is half the antenna. Leave a pi-match footprint, measure with the device in its final enclosure with the battery installed, and tune last. A small loop's radiation resistance is in milliohms, so copper loss and capacitor ESR set its efficiency; use the highest-Q tuning capacitor you can find.",
 use=(["The Chu limit: Qmin = 1/(ka)³ + 1/ka.", "Quarter-wave monopole over ground, about 36 Ω.", "Horn gain from aperture area and efficiency."], ["Wheeler's and McLean's refinements of the Chu limit."])),
14: dict(hook="Stand at one dish and see the other clearly through binoculars, and a 10 km link at 5.8 GHz can still fade. Radio doesn't travel on the thin line you see. It needs a fat ellipse around that line clear of obstacles, 11.4 m in radius at mid-path, and a ridge poking into it can take 11 dB out of the fade margin.",
 meet="Point-to-point microwave links, cell-site planning, rooftop installations, millimeter-wave rain fades, and every coverage prediction tool.",
 bench="Survey the path for 60 % of the first Fresnel zone, using the earth bulge for k = 4/3, and add clearance for trees that will grow. Above 10 GHz, check the rain region before you promise availability; a link that clears every hill can still drop in a thunderstorm. Raise the antennas rather than buying more power: height fixes a Fresnel-zone problem, while power only partly compensates for it.",
 use=(["Fresnel radius r1 = √(λ d1 d2/d) and 60 % clearance.", "Two-ray breakpoint 4 ht hr/λ.", "Rain and gas attenuation as added dB/km."], ["Full knife-edge diffraction integrals.", "ITU-R model derivations."])),
15: dict(hook="Disconnect the antenna from a transmitter at full power and the whole forward wave comes back at the output transistor. Put an isolator in the path and the transistor barely notices: with a 3:1 antenna and 20 dB of isolation, the amplifier sees a VSWR of about 1.1. A magnetized piece of ferrite does what no network of resistors, capacitors, and inductors can. It lets power through one way and not the other.",
 meet="Radar and base-station duplexers, isolators after power amplifiers, test benches that protect expensive sources, and satellite transponders.",
 bench="An isolator protects only as well as its termination survives. Rate the load for the full forward power, because an open antenna sends all of it there. Check the temperature range and the magnet's sensitivity to nearby steel; mounting an isolator next to a ferrous bracket can shift its band. In a circulator duplexer the antenna's match sets the transmit-to-receive leakage, so the antenna belongs in the isolation budget.",
 use=(["Port order 1 → 2 → 3, and the isolator as a circulator with one port terminated.", "Power into the termination: |Γ|² × forward power.", "Antenna return loss as part of transmit-receive isolation."], ["Polder tensor and gyromagnetic resonance theory."])),
16: dict(hook="A 1 m dish at 30 GHz isn't in its own far field until 200 m away. Measure it closer and the pattern you record isn't the pattern it radiates. That is why large high-frequency antennas are measured outdoors, in compact ranges, or by scanning the near field and computing the rest.",
 meet="Antenna data sheets, anechoic chambers, over-the-air certification tests, and the gain numbers in every link budget.",
 bench="Know where an antenna's gain figure came from: a calibrated standard horn, a three-antenna measurement, or a simulation with idealized materials. Indoors, check the far-field distance 2D²/λ first, then look for reflections from the floor, the mounting fixture, and the cables. Route the feed cable away from the antenna along a null and put ferrite on it; a radiating cable shield is the classic error in small-antenna measurements.",
 use=(["Far-field distance 2D²/λ.", "Gain by comparison with a standard horn.", "Gain from the two- or three-antenna Friis method."], ["Near-field to far-field transformation mathematics."])),
17: dict(hook="Half a decibel of cable sounds like nothing. Put it between a ground-station antenna and its LNA and the system noise temperature rises from 86 K to 125 K, about 1.7 dB worse. Every lossy part at room temperature adds its own noise to the signal, and in front of the first amplifier nothing else costs as much per decibel.",
 meet="Satellite ground stations, GPS antennas at the end of long cables, tower-mounted amplifiers on cell sites, and any receiver with a filter or switch before the LNA.",
 bench="Every decibel of loss in front of the LNA adds a decibel to the noise figure, so put the LNA as close to the antenna as its survival allows. That is why tower-mounted amplifiers and active GPS antennas exist. When filters and switches must come first for protection, choose them for insertion loss before anything else, and count the connector and trace losses too. Each is only a tenth of a decibel or so, which is why they get forgotten.",
 use=(["Te = (L − 1)·290 K for a lossy part at room temperature.", "Loss before an amplifier: NF_total = L + NF_amp, in dB.", "System temperature Tsys and kTB."], ["Noise-wave and noise-correlation matrix theory."])),
18: dict(hook="A filter specification often arrives as one line: three poles, 4 % bandwidth at 2.45 GHz, 0.1 dB ripple. By the end of this chapter that line has become two coupling coefficients, an external Q, a rejection figure at every frequency that matters, and a loss estimate the system engineer can put in the budget.",
 meet="Every custom band-pass filter, from a ceramic block in a phone to a cavity on a tower, and the conversation between a filter vendor and the system designer.",
 bench="Specify a filter by what the system needs: passband, loss, rejection at named frequencies, power, and temperature, and let the vendor pick the order. Ask for measured data over temperature, not just at 25 °C, and for the rejection far out of band, where spurious resonances and package parasitics can bring the response back up. Leave room for tuning; most narrowband filters are adjusted after assembly, by hand or by machine.",
 use=(["External Q and coupling coefficients from the prototype values.", "Rejection from the Chebyshev formula at an offset frequency.", "Midband loss from Qu, as in Chapter 6."], ["Coupling-matrix synthesis with cross-couplings."])),
19: dict(hook="An RF switch that is 'off' still has 0.1 pF across its open contacts. At 2.45 GHz that tiny capacitance limits isolation to about 16 dB, which won't protect a receiver from its own transmitter. Real switches add a second element that shorts the leftover signal to ground when the series element opens.",
 meet="Antenna and band selection in every phone, transmit/receive switching in TDD radios, step attenuators, and automated test systems.",
 bench="Read three switch numbers together: insertion loss, isolation at your highest frequency, and power handling at your worst VSWR, because the data-sheet power rating usually assumes a perfect match. Watch switching time and the settling of insertion phase in TDD systems. If the switch carries transmit power, check its harmonics and intermodulation; a cheap switch can generate spurs that fail a regulatory test.",
 use=(["Series switch loss and isolation from R_on and C_off.", "Series-shunt pairs for high isolation.", "Power handling derated for mismatch."], ["PIN-diode charge-storage models."])),
20: dict(hook="One decibel lost in the cable after a 100 W amplifier burns 20.6 W as heat and buys nothing. Recovering that decibel with a bigger amplifier costs far more than recovering it with a better cable. That arithmetic runs through every transmitter, from a phone's power amplifier to a radar with sixteen combined modules.",
 meet="Transmitter line-ups, EIRP limits in regulatory filings, base-station feeder runs, and the choice between GaN, LDMOS, GaAs, and SiGe devices.",
 bench="Draw the level diagram before anything else, with dBm at every stage from the modem to the antenna. It shows where power is lost, which stage compresses first, and how much margin each stage has. A decibel saved after the final amplifier is the cheapest decibel in the system. Count combining losses honestly: going from eight modules to sixteen adds another combining stage, and its loss, along with the modules.",
 use=(["EIRP = P_out − losses + antenna gain.", "dBm to watts in your head.", "Module count from required power and combining loss."], ["Device physics of each semiconductor family in depth."])),
}
